# grep website configuration.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Astro exposes anything prefixed PUBLIC_ to the browser and keeps everything
# else on the server. That split matters here: the OAuth client ID has to reach
# the browser to start sign-in, while the API URL used for server rendering does
# not have to.

# ------------------------------------------------- the admin service

# Where grep-backend is reachable from this server. Used when rendering pages
# to fetch published editions, and to mirror subscribers. Leave it unset and the
# site simply shows its two built-in editions - nothing breaks.
GREP_API_URL=http://localhost:8080

# The same address, as the browser sees it. The /admin page calls the service
# directly from the browser, so this must be reachable from outside your
# network - not `localhost` in production.
PUBLIC_GREP_API_URL=http://localhost:8080

# The OAuth client ID from the Google Cloud console. Must be the exact same
# value as GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID in grep-backend/.env, or sign-in will fail with a
# token-audience error. See grep-backend/README.md.
PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# ------------------------------------------------- mailing list (optional)

# One of these, if you have a provider. With neither set the form still works
# and the address is logged.
# BUTTONDOWN_API_KEY=
# SUBSCRIBE_WEBHOOK_URL=
# SUBSCRIBE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
